      "source_text": "Market expectations for the Fed’s policy rate in 2026 have undergone a significant shift – from pricing over two cuts at the start of the year to pricing in nearly two hikes by year end as of July. In recent weeks, markets have adjusted those expectations down to price in roughly one hike by end-2026 (in line with our expectations) in response to softer inflation and economic data. Investors are concerned about the impact of additional rate hikes on the economy and markets, but we see the effects as already largely priced in. Across various asset classes such as equities, Treasury yields, credit spreads and the U.S. dollar, the magnitude of their peak-to-trough moves since mid-June (-4.0% in the S&P 500, +40bps on the 10-year Treasury yield, +8bps in investment grade credit spreads, and +2.7% in the dollar index) are largely in-line with previous episodes of rate hikes in 2018 and 2022.",
